-- Q4717: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 105-10-05 and SFAC No. 8, paragraphs QC20-QC25, comparability enables users to identify similarities and differences across entities and periods. Changing the depreciation method disrupts period-to-period trend analysis because prior statements used a different method, requiring ASC 250-10-45 disclosures.
Wrong (A): Verifiability under ASC 105-10-05 (QC26) means independent observers could reach consensus on the figures. The analyst''s difficulty stems from inconsistent methods, not inability to verify amounts.
Wrong (B): Timeliness (QC29) means information is available soon enough to influence decisions. The issue here is cross-period inconsistency, not delayed reporting.
Wrong (D): Understandability (QC30) means information is comprehensible to informed users. The depreciation change does not make statements harder to understand, but harder to compare across periods.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4717;

-- Q4720: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 105-10-05 and SFAC No. 8, paragraph QC8, information has predictive value if it can be used as input to processes employed by users to predict future outcomes. Prior-year revenue figures feeding forward-looking forecasts directly exemplify this relevance component.
Wrong (A): Historical cost confirming the original purchase price illustrates confirmatory value under ASC 105-10-05 (QC9), not predictive value, because it validates a prior expectation rather than informing future projections.
Wrong (B): Verifying inventory counts demonstrates verifiability (QC26), an enhancing characteristic, not the predictive component of relevance.
Wrong (C): Disclosing accounting policies supports understandability and transparency but does not itself illustrate predictive value as a component of relevance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4720;

-- Q4722: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 105-10-05 and SFAC No. 5, paragraphs 63-70, recognition requires that an item (1) meet the definition of a financial statement element and (2) have a relevant attribute measurable with sufficient reliability. Both criteria must be satisfied before an item is recognized in the body of the financial statements.
Wrong (A): Foreign currency denomination is not a recognition criterion under ASC 105-10-05; an item may be recognized regardless of the currency in which it is denominated.
Wrong (B): Footnote disclosure and board approval are not recognition criteria under the Conceptual Framework; disclosure supplements but does not substitute for recognition.
Wrong (D): Materiality is a pervasive constraint, and SEC authorization is not a recognition criterion under ASC 105-10-05. Recognition depends on element definition and measurability, not regulatory approval.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4722;

-- Q4725: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 105-10-05 and SFAC No. 5, paragraph 67b, current replacement cost is the cash or equivalent that would be required to acquire the same or an equivalent asset today. This measurement basis reflects current market conditions for acquiring equivalent service capacity.
Wrong (A): Historical cost records the original acquisition price and is not updated for changes in current market conditions under ASC 105-10-05, so it does not reflect current replacement requirements.
Wrong (C): Present value of future cash flows measures the discounted expected inflows or outflows, reflecting exit or use value rather than the current cost to acquire an equivalent asset.
Wrong (D): Net realizable value is the estimated selling price less completion and disposal costs under ASC 330, representing an exit concept rather than the entry-price concept of replacement cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4725;

